// TEST: ML Equiv Restrict No Upward Leak (rejects)
import ml as ml
// ============================================================================
// RESTRICTION IS A ONE-WAY STREET, AND THIS IS THE DIRECTION THAT MUST FAIL
// (docs/plan-equivariance-in-types.md stage A3.)
//
// Stage A3 ships the branching rule: an O(3) representation space HAS a
// point-group decomposition, and `ml.pg_restrict` names it (corpus 096, 097).
// The temptation the table creates is to read that as a two-way bridge — if
// the C4 module is right there inside the O(3) one, surely a C4 theorem says
// something about the O(3) one? It does not, and it must not, because
// restriction along C4 -> O(3) THROWS INFORMATION AWAY:
//
//   * it forgets PARITY entirely (C4 is embedded as a proper rotation group,
//     so no element is improper and a pseudoscalar restricts to the trivial
//     label — corpus 096 section 3);
//   * it forgets every rotation that is not one of the four.
//
// So a C4-equivariance proof is a strictly weaker statement, and a checker that
// let it discharge an `ml.equiv(O3)` pin would be certifying a theorem nobody
// proved. The body below is the cleanest witness: it scales ONE axis.
// ============================================================================
let static V = [(1, 1, 1)]

// --- THE MAP THAT SEPARATES THE TWO GROUPS ----------------------------------
// The real basis of an l = 1 block is ordered m = -1, 0, +1 = (y, z, x), so
// index 1 IS the z component — the axis of the C4 embedding. Doubling it and
// leaving the (x, y) plane alone is:
//
//   * equivariant under EVERY rotation about z, hence in particular under all
//     four elements of C4 — the same map, written in the restricted layout
//     where the invariant cell moves to index 0, is `scale_invariant` in
//     corpus 096 section 5, and the engine discharges it there;
//   * NOT equivariant under O(3), nor even SO(3): rotating z into x turns the
//     doubled axis into an undoubled one.
//
// Composition refuses the body for its raw component reads, and the refusal is
// sound but uninformative. The 𝔰𝔬(3) engine then answers the SEMANTIC question
// and replaces it: the body IS a polynomial, and the identity
// Df(x)(A x) = A f(x) fails at a generator that mixes z with the plane. BL4008.
//
// The failing generator is the whole content of the rejection. There is no
// weaker pin that saves this function either: `where ml.equiv(SO3)` fails at
// the same generator (the defect is infinitesimal, not a parity slip), and
// `where ml.equiv(C4)` is refused one step earlier, at the SIGNATURE, because
// an IrrepsIdx parameter under a point-group certificate is corpus 048 — the
// branching table names the restricted space but does not relabel the buffer.
function stretch_z(v: Array<Float like IrrepsIdx<V>>)
                   where ml.equiv(O3) -> Array<Float like IrrepsIdx<V>> =
    [v(0), 2.0 * v(1), v(2)]

let out = stretch_z([1.0, 2.0, 3.0])
